WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.895
This is the executive
briefing for Book 11 of the

3
00:00:03.895 --> 00:00:07.812
Federal Modernization Workbook,
Zero Trust and Assessment.

4
00:00:07.812 --> 00:00:12.911
It runs about thirty minutes, and it's for
the people who make the calls: the CISO,

5
00:00:12.911 --> 00:00:17.775
the authorizing officials, the CIO,
and the Zero Trust implementation lead.

6
00:00:17.775 --> 00:00:20.487
The argument fits in three sentences.

7
00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:26.088
Zero Trust maturity is a property of the
whole agency, not of any product or tenant.

8
00:00:26.088 --> 00:00:30.226
The federal documents set specific
actions and configurations,

9
00:00:30.226 --> 00:00:34.749
but none of them defines a Zero Trust
score or requires a maturity stage.

10
00:00:34.749 --> 00:00:37.591
And the assessment tools
produce evidence for

11
00:00:37.591 --> 00:00:40.287
named people to act on,
not verdicts to report.

12
00:00:40.287 --> 00:00:44.855
I'll take four questions in order: why
Zero Trust belongs to the whole agency,

13
00:00:44.855 --> 00:00:48.906
what the mandates and tools actually
require, who owns the posture,

14
00:00:48.906 --> 00:00:53.325
and how to turn findings into a program
the authorizing official can see.

15
00:00:53.325 --> 00:00:57.875
Every decision slide names the decision,
its owner, and when it's needed.

16
00:00:57.875 --> 00:01:00.900
Let's start with the
usual assessment calendar.

17
00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.675
Here's how assessment usually works.

18
00:01:06.675 --> 00:01:10.200
The network team reviews the
boundary, keyed by address.

19
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:15.200
The cloud team runs each provider's
posture scan, keyed by resource tags.

20
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.675
The virtualization team
works a hypervisor checklist.

21
00:01:18.675 --> 00:01:22.104
At year end someone puts
the three reports behind one

22
00:01:22.104 --> 00:01:25.688
cover page and calls it the
agency's Zero Trust maturity.

23
00:01:25.688 --> 00:01:28.424
It's three partial
assessments with one title.

24
00:01:28.424 --> 00:01:31.725
The failure is architectural,
not organizational.

25
00:01:31.725 --> 00:01:34.323
A policy decision can only treat a user,

26
00:01:34.323 --> 00:01:39.925
a device and a resource consistently if
every platform describes them the same way.

27
00:01:39.925 --> 00:01:45.200
One database server can show up as three
unrelated objects, scored by three tools.

28
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.600
A total across them counts
objects, not architecture.

29
00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.113
The federal guidance describes
the enterprise, not a tenant.

30
00:01:52.113 --> 00:01:55.903
Executive Order 14028, M-22-09,

31
00:01:55.903 --> 00:02:01.425
CISA's maturity model and NIST
all speak in agency-wide terms.

32
00:02:01.425 --> 00:02:06.150
A scan can confirm that an architecture
is unified; it can't make it unified.

33
00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:11.237
So the first decision: reconcile the
network, every cloud and the virtualization

34
00:02:11.237 --> 00:02:16.400
estate to one authoritative asset and
identity record before scoring any of them.

35
00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:20.975
The book gives that to the owner of the
naming and identity plane, this quarter.

36
00:02:20.975 --> 00:02:23.590
Until it's done, report any whole-agency

37
00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:26.613
figure as partial
assessments, not one score.

38
00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:30.990
Next, why this is a security
issue, not a reporting preference.

39
00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:37.365
Two documented incidents
show why this is a

40
00:02:37.365 --> 00:02:40.428
security property and not
a reporting preference.

41
00:02:40.428 --> 00:02:44.417
In 2023, an attacker used a
Microsoft consumer signing

42
00:02:44.417 --> 00:02:48.052
key to forge tokens and
read Exchange Online mail.

43
00:02:48.052 --> 00:02:52.785
The Cyber Safety Review Board counted
22 enterprise organizations affected,

44
00:02:52.785 --> 00:02:55.115
and called the intrusion preventable.

45
00:02:55.115 --> 00:02:58.740
The forged tokens were valid to
every system that checked them.

46
00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:01.419
What caught it was a
custom alert one federal

47
00:03:01.419 --> 00:03:04.315
department had built on
mailbox logs it paid for.

48
00:03:04.315 --> 00:03:08.165
The agency's own evidence found what
the provider's platform didn't.

49
00:03:08.165 --> 00:03:10.227
The second sits beneath the guest.

50
00:03:10.227 --> 00:03:14.019
In February 2023, the ESXiArgs campaign

51
00:03:14.019 --> 00:03:18.615
compromised more than
3,800 VMware ESXi servers.

52
00:03:18.615 --> 00:03:21.556
And Microsoft noted that
many security products

53
00:03:21.556 --> 00:03:24.640
have limited visibility
into the hypervisor at all.

54
00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:27.590
Both cases make the same
point from opposite ends.

55
00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:31.327
The worst failures happened where no
single platform's tool was looking.

56
00:03:31.327 --> 00:03:33.690
There's a consequence for the fix, too.

57
00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:37.958
Once one plane names and identifies
everything the agency runs,

58
00:03:37.958 --> 00:03:40.615
it's a complete map of the attack surface.

59
00:03:40.615 --> 00:03:45.784
So the decision: govern that unified plane
as a Tier 0 asset from the day it exists,

60
00:03:45.784 --> 00:03:49.779
with time-boxed administration,
its own monitored telemetry,

61
00:03:49.779 --> 00:03:52.677
and a design that assumes
it will be attacked.

62
00:03:52.677 --> 00:03:56.902
Identity engineering and
the DNS and DDI team own it.

63
00:03:56.902 --> 00:03:59.090
Next, why programs stall.

64
00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:07.427
Programs stall low on CISA's maturity
model because they read it as a

65
00:04:07.427 --> 00:04:12.578
five-pillar shopping list: identity,
devices, networks, applications and data.

66
00:04:12.578 --> 00:04:17.456
But the model runs those pillars through
three cross-cutting capabilities, and the

67
00:04:17.456 --> 00:04:22.477
one programs fund least is governance:
defining and enforcing policy across pillars.

68
00:04:22.477 --> 00:04:24.790
No single pillar's tool delivers that.

69
00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:26.740
Three failure patterns recur.

70
00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:29.227
Pillars are administered, not governed.

71
00:04:29.227 --> 00:04:33.234
Evidence is shaped by each tool
rather than by the claim it supports,

72
00:04:33.234 --> 00:04:35.666
so every assessment starts from scratch.

73
00:04:35.666 --> 00:04:39.190
And drift is found at audit
time, months after it opened.

74
00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:41.765
The book's answer rests
on a few principles.

75
00:04:41.765 --> 00:04:45.424
Identity is the anchor, but
it isn't self-authenticating,

76
00:04:45.424 --> 00:04:49.640
so sessions are re-evaluated and
the identity plane is defended.

77
00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:52.528
Privileged identity gets
controls of its own.

78
00:04:52.528 --> 00:04:55.815
Evidence must be reproducible
and machine-readable.

79
00:04:55.815 --> 00:04:58.877
And governance must
enforce, not only observe.

80
00:04:58.877 --> 00:05:03.452
A policy whose only consequence is
next quarter's finding is a report.

81
00:05:03.452 --> 00:05:07.703
The decision is the CISO's, with
identity engineering, this quarter:

82
00:05:07.703 --> 00:05:11.724
time-box privileged access,
including the assessment accounts,

83
00:05:11.724 --> 00:05:15.291
and convert one detective
control into a preventive one.

84
00:05:15.291 --> 00:05:19.598
Microsoft's own guidance is zero
permanently active admin assignments,

85
00:05:19.598 --> 00:05:24.552
other than emergency accounts that are
monitored and tested at least every 90 days.

86
00:05:24.552 --> 00:05:27.570
Next, what the mandates actually require.

87
00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:34.468
Zero Trust programs are
usually argued by citation,

88
00:05:34.468 --> 00:05:37.470
and most of the citations
fail when you check them.

89
00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:40.133
Four documents get cited
as if they were one.

90
00:05:40.133 --> 00:05:42.915
Executive Order 14028 is binding,

91
00:05:42.915 --> 00:05:47.983
and required each agency to plan
for Zero Trust Architecture.

92
00:05:47.983 --> 00:05:53.257
OMB M-22-09 is binding, and sets
specific actions by pillar.

93
00:05:53.257 --> 00:05:57.233
CISA's maturity model is guidance;
it says it shouldn't be viewed

94
00:05:57.233 --> 00:06:01.358
as a strict set of requirements,
and its page is now marked archived.

95
00:06:01.358 --> 00:06:05.507
NIST SP 800-207 is guidance too.

96
00:06:05.507 --> 00:06:10.009
M-22-09 borrows the model's
pillars to organize its actions,

97
00:06:10.009 --> 00:06:12.832
but doesn't adopt its
stages as requirements.

98
00:06:12.832 --> 00:06:16.520
That leaves several requirements
that circulate but don't exist.

99
00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:21.132
No federal document requires a particular
maturity stage, such as Advanced.

100
00:06:21.132 --> 00:06:24.296
No federal document
defines a Zero Trust score,

101
00:06:24.296 --> 00:06:29.732
so a tool that claims to certify maturity
is citing a requirement that isn't there.

102
00:06:29.732 --> 00:06:36.157
And M-22-09 doesn't require
ScubaGear; that comes from BOD 25-01.

103
00:06:36.157 --> 00:06:38.058
None of that makes the target vague.

104
00:06:38.058 --> 00:06:42.232
It means the target is a choice
the agency makes and has to defend.

105
00:06:42.232 --> 00:06:45.870
So the decision: set a maturity
target for each pillar,

106
00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:48.845
written down as program
design, not mandate.

107
00:06:48.845 --> 00:06:52.638
The Zero Trust program lead
decides it, consulting the CIO,

108
00:06:52.638 --> 00:06:57.695
the CISO and the authorizing official,
before the next assessment is scored.

109
00:06:57.695 --> 00:07:00.710
Next, the one memo that still binds.

110
00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:08.386
M-22-09 is the only one of
these that sets dated actions

111
00:07:08.386 --> 00:07:11.598
for the program itself, and it
isn't limited to the cloud.

112
00:07:11.598 --> 00:07:14.710
It covers on-premises
and hybrid systems too.

113
00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:16.373
Its actions are specific.

114
00:07:16.373 --> 00:07:20.458
Phishing-resistant MFA for
staff, contractors and partners,

115
00:07:20.458 --> 00:07:24.789
which means agency systems stop
supporting SMS and voice codes,

116
00:07:24.789 --> 00:07:28.711
one-time codes and push
notifications for routine access.

117
00:07:28.711 --> 00:07:32.260
Reliable device inventories
and endpoint detection.

118
00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:36.275
Encrypted DNS, HTTPS for
all web and API traffic,

119
00:07:36.275 --> 00:07:40.598
and moving away from one
broad enterprise network.

120
00:07:40.598 --> 00:07:44.398
Security testing for applications,
and comprehensive logging.

121
00:07:44.398 --> 00:07:47.560
Its fiscal 2024 deadlines have passed.

122
00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:52.547
But OMB hasn't rescinded it, and a passed
deadline doesn't retire an action.

123
00:07:52.547 --> 00:07:55.626
Agencies are now measured
against these actions as

124
00:07:55.626 --> 00:07:58.561
an existing obligation
rather than a target date.

125
00:07:58.561 --> 00:07:59.848
There's one adjustment.

126
00:07:59.848 --> 00:08:06.574
The logging action pointed to M-21-31,
which M-26-14 rescinded on May 22, 2026,

127
00:08:06.574 --> 00:08:13.860
replacing it with a risk-based approach
and a Logging Reference Architecture.

128
00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:17.685
CISA published that architecture
on August 20, 2026,

129
00:08:17.685 --> 00:08:23.335
and agencies have 90 days from its release
for their first Agency Logging Plan.

130
00:08:23.335 --> 00:08:25.623
That's the decision with the nearest date.

131
00:08:25.623 --> 00:08:29.723
The CISO decides how the logging
plan covers Zero Trust telemetry,

132
00:08:29.723 --> 00:08:33.311
including the hosts and accounts
that run the assessments.

133
00:08:33.311 --> 00:08:37.235
It's due 90 days after August 20, 2026.

134
00:08:37.235 --> 00:08:40.830
Next, BOD 25-01.

135
00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:48.455
BOD 25-01 is narrower and more concrete.

136
00:08:48.455 --> 00:08:53.692
CISA issued it on December 17, 2024,
and it binds federal civilian agencies

137
00:08:53.692 --> 00:08:59.267
for every production cloud tenant of a
product with a finalized SCuBA baseline.

138
00:08:59.267 --> 00:09:01.119
It has six required actions,

139
00:09:01.119 --> 00:09:06.753
from inventorying tenants and deploying
the assessment tools to implementing the

140
00:09:06.753 --> 00:09:09.088
mandatory policies by June 20, 2025,

141
00:09:09.088 --> 00:09:13.756
and baselining every new tenant
before its authorization to operate.

142
00:09:13.756 --> 00:09:20.455
The mandatory list is a subset of the
baselines: 50 policies for Microsoft 365.

143
00:09:20.455 --> 00:09:24.654
And the scope is software as a
service only, so Azure subscriptions,

144
00:09:24.654 --> 00:09:29.005
on-premises Active Directory,
endpoints and networks are untouched.

145
00:09:29.005 --> 00:09:31.242
Google Workspace is now in scope.

146
00:09:31.242 --> 00:09:37.455
Its tenant inventory is due November
2, 2026, and the tools by December 1.

147
00:09:37.455 --> 00:09:42.293
CISA's pages disagree on the policy
date, so plan to December 1.

148
00:09:42.293 --> 00:09:47.105
Even a small tenant used by one
component carries all six obligations.

149
00:09:47.105 --> 00:09:48.380
Now count honestly.

150
00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:50.905
Inventory and reporting take days.

151
00:09:50.905 --> 00:09:54.247
Implementing and holding the
policies is architecture,

152
00:09:54.247 --> 00:09:56.593
and that's what the directive exists for.

153
00:09:56.593 --> 00:10:00.294
So measure compliance by how
many mandatory policies hold,

154
00:10:00.294 --> 00:10:04.298
and whether every open deviation
is either being fixed on a date

155
00:10:04.298 --> 00:10:08.680
or carried as the authorizing
official's documented risk acceptance.

156
00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:11.739
The CISO owns the inventory and reporting,

157
00:10:11.739 --> 00:10:14.967
and the Workspace inventory
is due November 2.

158
00:10:14.967 --> 00:10:16.680
Next, reporting.

159
00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:23.005
Reporting is where many agencies stopped,

160
00:10:23.005 --> 00:10:26.830
so it's worth being precise about
what CISA actually receives.

161
00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:30.068
CISA's guidance describes
four reporting routes,

162
00:10:30.068 --> 00:10:35.543
from a CISA-hosted service that feeds the
CDM dashboard to quarterly manual uploads.

163
00:10:35.543 --> 00:10:39.869
Whatever the route, the reported
artifact is the tool's own results file,

164
00:10:39.869 --> 00:10:42.142
and agencies are told not to rename it.

165
00:10:42.142 --> 00:10:47.200
A deviation that's operationally required
shows up as Omitted, with its rationale,

166
00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:51.142
and must be approved through the
agency's security risk process.

167
00:10:51.142 --> 00:10:55.451
CISA also supplies a template for
tracking non-compliant policies,

168
00:10:55.451 --> 00:10:57.417
planned fixes and target dates.

169
00:10:57.417 --> 00:11:00.681
In effect, it's a plan of
action and milestones.

170
00:11:00.681 --> 00:11:03.968
The directive puts the risk
decision in the right place.

171
00:11:03.968 --> 00:11:09.236
Authorizing officials may accept risk for
deviations from the mandatory policies

172
00:11:09.236 --> 00:11:14.354
to meet operational needs, under agency
policy, and CISA reviews what they submit.

173
00:11:14.354 --> 00:11:15.805
Two decisions follow.

174
00:11:15.805 --> 00:11:19.993
The CISO picks the reporting route,
and the assessment operators run it.

175
00:11:19.993 --> 00:11:23.729
Operators never edit results,
and never decide omissions.

176
00:11:23.729 --> 00:11:28.229
And every deviation without an end
date goes to the authorizing official,

177
00:11:28.229 --> 00:11:31.843
because a standing omission is
a standing risk acceptance.

178
00:11:31.843 --> 00:11:34.378
The reporting floor is at least quarterly,

179
00:11:34.378 --> 00:11:37.493
so both are needed before
the next quarterly report.

180
00:11:37.493 --> 00:11:40.070
Next, the two assessment tools.

181
00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:48.700
Agencies can run two free, read-only
assessments of their Microsoft cloud,

182
00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:51.820
and often drop one because
both cover identity.

183
00:11:51.820 --> 00:11:53.495
They answer different questions.

184
00:11:53.495 --> 00:11:55.520
ScubaGear is CISA's tool.

185
00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:57.754
It asks a compliance question:

186
00:11:57.754 --> 00:12:02.132
does this Microsoft 365 tenant
meet the federal baselines?

187
00:12:02.132 --> 00:12:06.733
It's the tool the directive names,
and its output is what CISA receives.

188
00:12:06.733 --> 00:12:10.708
Microsoft's Zero Trust Assessment
asks a maturity question:

189
00:12:10.708 --> 00:12:14.445
how close is the environment
to Microsoft's own guidance?

190
00:12:14.445 --> 00:12:18.538
It's a community project
maintained by Microsoft employees,

191
00:12:18.538 --> 00:12:22.795
not an official product, and
it isn't a BOD 25-01 tool.

192
00:12:22.795 --> 00:12:24.907
They overlap mostly on identity.

193
00:12:24.907 --> 00:12:28.291
Only ScubaGear covers
Exchange Online, Teams,

194
00:12:28.291 --> 00:12:33.633
SharePoint and Power Platform, where
22 of the 50 mandatory policies sit.

195
00:12:33.633 --> 00:12:36.329
Drop ScubaGear and mandatory policies go

196
00:12:36.329 --> 00:12:39.257
unassessed and the
required reporting stops.

197
00:12:39.257 --> 00:12:43.369
Drop the other, and devices,
network and data go unmeasured.

198
00:12:43.369 --> 00:12:47.508
Even running both is one vendor's
answer about one vendor's platform.

199
00:12:47.508 --> 00:12:51.232
Neither sees another cloud,
the network or the hypervisors.

200
00:12:51.232 --> 00:12:54.070
And a score is a reading,
not a treatment plan.

201
00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:58.403
The CISO decides, before the next
production run: approve both tools,

202
00:12:58.403 --> 00:13:03.421
with pinned versions, and keep their
output inside the authorization boundary,

203
00:13:03.421 --> 00:13:06.158
because it's a map of
where the tenant is weak.

204
00:13:06.158 --> 00:13:10.783
Expect the score to move when a version
changes, even if the tenant didn't.

205
00:13:10.783 --> 00:13:14.930
Next, everything beyond Microsoft 365.

206
00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:24.242
Everything so far stops at the edge of
Microsoft 365 and Google Workspace.

207
00:13:24.242 --> 00:13:28.926
Nothing on BOD 25-01's list
covers infrastructure accounts,

208
00:13:28.926 --> 00:13:31.892
the agency network or
the virtualized estate.

209
00:13:31.892 --> 00:13:35.804
So the whole-agency argument
rests on the Zero Trust documents,

210
00:13:35.804 --> 00:13:39.792
and on the controls every system
security plan already asserts.

211
00:13:39.792 --> 00:13:43.405
Three things get reconciled before
those estates are assessed.

212
00:13:43.405 --> 00:13:46.680
One inventory, with no
component counted twice.

213
00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:48.261
One naming authority,

214
00:13:48.261 --> 00:13:53.005
because each cloud provider and
VMware ships its own private DNS.

215
00:13:53.005 --> 00:13:57.172
And no implicit trust from any
boundary: a site-to-site VPN and

216
00:13:57.172 --> 00:14:01.755
a flat virtualized segment are
architecturally the same mistake.

217
00:14:01.755 --> 00:14:04.632
The hypervisor is a
threat class of its own,

218
00:14:04.632 --> 00:14:08.330
so its management plane is
assessed as its own surface.

219
00:14:08.330 --> 00:14:12.268
Then the providers' own tools, each
of which states its own limits.

220
00:14:12.268 --> 00:14:15.956
AWS says its checks cover
a subset of requirements.

221
00:14:15.956 --> 00:14:19.580
Microsoft calls Azure Policy
compliance a partial view.

222
00:14:19.580 --> 00:14:23.668
Broadcom calls its VMware hardening
material general guidance.

223
00:14:23.668 --> 00:14:26.267
And FedRAMP certifies the cloud service,

224
00:14:26.267 --> 00:14:29.555
while each agency still
authorizes its own systems.

225
00:14:29.555 --> 00:14:31.729
The decision is the program lead's:

226
00:14:31.729 --> 00:14:35.998
admit each native tool's findings
as evidence for its own platform,

227
00:14:35.998 --> 00:14:40.268
under the CISO's evidence standard,
and never as the agency's score.

228
00:14:40.268 --> 00:14:43.630
It's needed before any
whole-agency figure is reported.

229
00:14:43.630 --> 00:14:46.030
Next, who owns the posture.

230
00:14:49.780 --> 00:14:53.318
Zero Trust programs rarely
fail for lack of owners.

231
00:14:53.318 --> 00:14:56.255
They fail because every
owner is a pillar owner.

232
00:14:56.255 --> 00:15:01.123
Nobody owns the question this book keeps
asking: what is the agency's posture across

233
00:15:01.123 --> 00:15:05.567
all five pillars, and what does the number
on the cover page actually measure?

234
00:15:05.567 --> 00:15:11.180
The CIO and executive sponsor set strategy,
scope and funding, and break ties.

235
00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:16.401
Three CIO decisions matter here: which
estates the whole-agency score covers,

236
00:15:16.401 --> 00:15:20.968
in writing, so it can't quietly shrink
to the estates that assess well;

237
00:15:20.968 --> 00:15:25.618
the sequence of work; and which team
owns a control that spans pillars.

238
00:15:25.618 --> 00:15:30.224
The Zero Trust program lead owns the
posture: the definition of the score,

239
00:15:30.224 --> 00:15:33.755
the assessment calendar and
the prioritized worklist.

240
00:15:33.755 --> 00:15:39.380
Many agencies will give this to the
implementation lead M-22-09 required.

241
00:15:39.380 --> 00:15:42.421
The lead decides priority
but doesn't accept risk,

242
00:15:42.421 --> 00:15:47.242
and shouldn't also own the agency's
relationship with its largest cloud vendor.

243
00:15:47.242 --> 00:15:49.681
That independence is
the point of the role.

244
00:15:49.681 --> 00:15:53.935
The CISO sets the evidence standard,
and the authorizing officials

245
00:15:53.935 --> 00:15:57.817
accept residual risk, each only
for the systems they authorize.

246
00:15:57.817 --> 00:16:00.234
The decision is the CIO's, this quarter:

247
00:16:00.234 --> 00:16:03.585
name an agency-level owner
of the Zero Trust posture,

248
00:16:03.585 --> 00:16:09.118
and a cross-pillar correlation owner, separate
from any cloud provider relationship.

249
00:16:09.118 --> 00:16:11.340
Next, the pillar owners.

250
00:16:15.090 --> 00:16:19.248
The book maps each of CISA's five
pillars to the team that owns it after

251
00:16:19.248 --> 00:16:22.904
modernization: identity
engineering, endpoint engineering,

252
00:16:22.904 --> 00:16:25.915
network engineering with
security architecture,

253
00:16:25.915 --> 00:16:29.715
application owners with the platform
teams, and the data owners.

254
00:16:29.715 --> 00:16:34.044
Across them, security operations
owns visibility, and governance,

255
00:16:34.044 --> 00:16:36.798
the part no pillar
owner can deliver alone,

256
00:16:36.798 --> 00:16:41.127
sits with the program lead, the
CISO and the authorizing official.

257
00:16:41.127 --> 00:16:42.639
One rule runs through it.

258
00:16:42.639 --> 00:16:45.202
Pillar owners decide how to fix a finding.

259
00:16:45.202 --> 00:16:47.515
They don't decide that
it doesn't need fixing.

260
00:16:47.515 --> 00:16:50.278
And three calls often
land on the wrong desk.

261
00:16:50.278 --> 00:16:53.761
An exception with an expiration
date is a CISO decision,

262
00:16:53.761 --> 00:16:56.652
recorded in the versioned
configuration file.

263
00:16:56.652 --> 00:17:00.620
An exception with no end date
is a standing risk acceptance,

264
00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:05.640
so it goes to the authorizing
official, which matches BOD 25-01.

265
00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:09.896
And drift, a control that passed every
interval and failed the last one,

266
00:17:09.896 --> 00:17:13.784
also goes to the authorizing
official, because it's a change to an

267
00:17:13.784 --> 00:17:18.040
authorized system, and the official
decides whether it's significant.

268
00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:20.695
So the decision for
the CISO, this quarter:

269
00:17:20.695 --> 00:17:25.627
move every exception out of documents
and into the configuration file and the

270
00:17:25.627 --> 00:17:27.599
repository, with an expiration,

271
00:17:27.599 --> 00:17:31.165
and route each standing one
to the authorizing official.

272
00:17:31.165 --> 00:17:33.200
Next, how to get there.

273
00:17:36.950 --> 00:17:37.975
How do you get there?

274
00:17:37.975 --> 00:17:40.300
First, close before you assess.

275
00:17:40.300 --> 00:17:45.588
Agencies met BOD 25-01's early
dates, with tenants inventoried

276
00:17:45.588 --> 00:17:49.937
and ScubaGear reporting, and many
now treat that as the answer.

277
00:17:49.937 --> 00:17:53.062
But the tools measure deviation;
they don't close any.

278
00:17:53.062 --> 00:17:57.688
A thermometer that reports a fever every
day for a year isn't a treatment plan.

279
00:17:57.688 --> 00:18:00.423
Without an architecture to absorb the fix,

280
00:18:00.423 --> 00:18:05.425
remediation collapses into re-running
the scan and filing another plan line.

281
00:18:05.425 --> 00:18:10.226
So the program's sequence puts the
assessors at step five, as confirmation,

282
00:18:10.226 --> 00:18:14.949
not discovery: understand the gaps,
design the fix, implement it, govern it,

283
00:18:14.949 --> 00:18:19.750
then validate, and then the authorizing
official decides on the evidence.

284
00:18:19.750 --> 00:18:22.450
That's the Risk Management
Framework's own order.

285
00:18:22.450 --> 00:18:24.963
Not every finding waits for architecture.

286
00:18:24.963 --> 00:18:29.571
Each gets exactly one response: a
configuration change that closes now,

287
00:18:29.571 --> 00:18:32.384
architectural work with
an owner and a date,

288
00:18:32.384 --> 00:18:35.587
or a risk acceptance by
the authorizing official.

289
00:18:35.587 --> 00:18:40.825
A finding with no response recorded is the
only state the program treats as failure.

290
00:18:40.825 --> 00:18:46.773
This is the CIO's sequence decision:
close the BOD 25-01 findings before the

291
00:18:46.773 --> 00:18:52.225
maturity assessment, and staff that order
against the agency's other deadlines.

292
00:18:52.225 --> 00:18:55.437
It's needed before the maturity
assessment is scheduled.

293
00:18:55.437 --> 00:18:57.960
Next, how to read the results.

294
00:19:01.710 --> 00:19:04.585
The two tools use different
words for the same outcomes,

295
00:19:04.585 --> 00:19:07.085
and some of their outcomes
aren't findings at all.

296
00:19:07.085 --> 00:19:11.011
Only four results are findings:
ScubaGear's Fail and Warning,

297
00:19:11.011 --> 00:19:14.622
and the Zero Trust Assessment's
Failed and Investigate.

298
00:19:14.622 --> 00:19:15.873
The rest need care.

299
00:19:15.873 --> 00:19:21.573
A not-applicable result is usually a manual
check to verify by hand, not a pass.

300
00:19:21.573 --> 00:19:25.160
An error means the run
failed: fix it and re-run.

301
00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:28.272
And a skipped check is
neither a gap nor a pass.

302
00:19:28.272 --> 00:19:30.069
Often the reason is a license,

303
00:19:30.069 --> 00:19:34.335
or a feature that isn't available or
authorized in a government cloud.

304
00:19:34.335 --> 00:19:37.071
Scoring it as a failure
overstates the gap;

305
00:19:37.071 --> 00:19:39.885
scoring it as a pass
overstates the posture.

306
00:19:39.885 --> 00:19:42.411
So it's recorded as absence of evidence,

307
00:19:42.411 --> 00:19:45.648
with the reason, and it
becomes a boundary finding.

308
00:19:45.648 --> 00:19:51.347
The program then ranks the real findings
with a documented method, P1 down to P4.

309
00:19:51.347 --> 00:19:55.200
That method is the program's,
not a federal standard,

310
00:19:55.200 --> 00:19:58.410
and a P1 is a priority,
not a risk acceptance.

311
00:19:58.410 --> 00:20:02.879
And findings are reconciled at the
control: two tools failing the same

312
00:20:02.879 --> 00:20:07.348
setting is one finding with two pieces
of evidence, routed to one owner.

313
00:20:07.348 --> 00:20:12.570
Underneath it all sits the CISO's
evidence standard: a pinned tool version,

314
00:20:12.570 --> 00:20:16.360
a recorded run time, an
unaltered output file and a hash.

315
00:20:16.360 --> 00:20:19.822
It's needed before any result
is scored or reported.

316
00:20:19.822 --> 00:20:22.960
Next, turning findings into a program.

317
00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:31.148
Now turn the worklist into a program
the authorizing official can see.

318
00:20:31.148 --> 00:20:35.596
Every finding that isn't fixed in the next
change window and isn't covered by an

319
00:20:35.596 --> 00:20:39.351
approved exception becomes a plan
of action and milestones item,

320
00:20:39.351 --> 00:20:41.784
which the authorizing official approves.

321
00:20:41.784 --> 00:20:44.102
Each item names the owner, the control,

322
00:20:44.102 --> 00:20:48.810
the evidence that will show it closed,
and the re-assessment that will test it.

323
00:20:48.810 --> 00:20:53.363
Behind those items, the program
seals each tool's unaltered output,

324
00:20:53.363 --> 00:20:57.585
with its provenance, into a bundle
that rebuilds byte for byte.

325
00:20:57.585 --> 00:21:00.221
An assessor can rebuild
it months later and

326
00:21:00.221 --> 00:21:02.710
walk back from a finding
to the raw result.

327
00:21:02.710 --> 00:21:06.322
That's the difference between an
archived opinion and evidence.

328
00:21:06.322 --> 00:21:10.900
Re-assessment runs on a calendar the
program lead owns, and after each

329
00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:15.798
remediation wave, so a finding is closed
by evidence, not by a ticket status.

330
00:21:15.798 --> 00:21:17.823
ScubaGear's floor is quarterly.

331
00:21:17.823 --> 00:21:22.671
And drift goes to the authorizing
official, not only to security operations,

332
00:21:22.671 --> 00:21:27.135
because a control that newly fails
is a change to an authorized system.

333
00:21:27.135 --> 00:21:30.972
The book calls that the cheapest
adoption step in its final chapter.

334
00:21:30.972 --> 00:21:34.009
So the decision sits with
the authorizing official:

335
00:21:34.009 --> 00:21:38.459
receive drift findings every assessment
interval, prepared by the program

336
00:21:38.459 --> 00:21:42.697
lead and security operations, and
decide which are significant changes.

337
00:21:42.697 --> 00:21:44.122
Stand it up this quarter.

338
00:21:44.122 --> 00:21:47.170
To close, the decisions in one place.

339
00:21:50.920 --> 00:21:53.620
So, the decisions, with their owners.

340
00:21:53.620 --> 00:21:55.382
None of them is a procurement.

341
00:21:55.382 --> 00:22:00.126
The CIO names an agency-level owner of
the Zero Trust posture and a cross-pillar

342
00:22:00.126 --> 00:22:04.212
correlation owner, separate from
any cloud provider relationship,

343
00:22:04.212 --> 00:22:07.496
and puts the scope of the
whole-agency score in writing.

344
00:22:07.496 --> 00:22:10.775
The identity-plane owner
reconciles the network,

345
00:22:10.775 --> 00:22:15.895
every cloud and the virtualization estate
to one record before any is scored.

346
00:22:15.895 --> 00:22:18.600
The program lead finds
out, pillar by pillar,

347
00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:22.057
which evidence can be re-run
without the operator's help.

348
00:22:22.057 --> 00:22:27.239
The CISO moves every exception into the
configuration file with an expiration,

349
00:22:27.239 --> 00:22:31.647
covers the assessment hosts and
accounts in the Agency Logging Plan,

350
00:22:31.647 --> 00:22:35.668
due 90 days after August 20,
brings any Google Workspace tenant

351
00:22:35.668 --> 00:22:39.921
into the inventory by November 2,
and time-boxes privileged access.

352
00:22:39.921 --> 00:22:43.973
The program lead and security
operations stand up drift detection,

353
00:22:43.973 --> 00:22:47.157
and the authorizing official
receives its findings.

354
00:22:47.157 --> 00:22:50.274
And every government-cloud
feature gap is recorded

355
00:22:50.274 --> 00:22:53.246
as a governed finding, not
an unstated assumption.

356
00:22:53.246 --> 00:22:55.920
Each is a structural step that compounds.

357
00:22:55.920 --> 00:22:57.596
Unify first, assess second,

358
00:22:57.596 --> 00:23:01.558
and let named people make the
decisions the evidence supports.

359
00:23:01.558 --> 00:23:06.183
No tool, and no number of tools,
certifies Zero Trust maturity.

360
00:23:06.183 --> 00:23:10.157
Book 11 has the detail,
with every claim footnoted.

361
00:23:10.157 --> 00:23:10.980
Thank you.
